#
# LeakSanitizer suppressions, for the asan trees.
#
#   ./build.sh linux asan
#   FNL_BACKEND=x11 LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=$PWD/lsan.supp \
#       ./build/linux/asan/fenestal-testbed
#
# stress.py sets the variable for an asan round, and bare_driver.py ships the
# file with the program. An entry matches a leak if any frame of its
# allocation's stack is in the named module, so an entry is only as good as
# the stack: malloc's fast unwinder stops at the first library built without
# frame pointers, which for a leak inside libxcb is libxcb itself. A driver
# that leaks through such a library is therefore suppressed only where the
# round also asks for the slow unwinder (fast_unwind_on_malloc=0), and
# bare_driver.py does exactly where it pins that driver. Never key an entry
# on libxcb, libX11 or libwayland-client: the backends allocate through
# them, and an entry there hides a reply this project forgot to free.
#
# Each entry says what report it was checked against, and why the stack it
# keys on cannot be one of this project's.

# ---- NVIDIA's own driver ---------------------------------------------------
# Checked against the x11-vulkan-bare asan round on schenker's GTX 1050 Ti
# (580.178.04, Xorg, no manager), full stacks: 746 to 790 bytes in five or
# six blocks at exit, and nothing else.
#   - vkCreateInstance -> libGLX_nvidia -> libnvidia-glcore ->
#     dbus_bus_register: the driver's D-Bus connection, kept by the driver
#     and never closed; three blocks, one direct.
#   - vkQueuePresentKHR -> libGLX_nvidia -> xcb_poll_for_special_event and
#     xcb_wait_for_special_event: Present's special events, read by the
#     driver's WSI and not freed; 44 bytes each.
# The ICD is closed, so no frame inside it is named, and the module is the
# narrowest key there is. This project gives the driver no allocation
# callbacks, so nothing of ours is allocated under one of its frames.
leak:libGLX_nvidia.so
leak:libnvidia-glcore.so
#
# And the same D-Bus connection made through NVIDIA's EGL. Checked against
# the kms-gl-bare asan round on schenker's GTX 1050 Ti (580.178.04, no
# window server): eglCreateContext -> libEGL_nvidia -> (the ICD's own
# frames, unnamed) -> dbus_bus_register, 184 bytes direct and 374
# indirect at exit, and nothing else. aspire's 960M on the same driver did
# not report it in its round, which says when, not whether. The module is
# the narrowest key for the reason above, and EGL is given no allocator of
# ours either.
leak:libEGL_nvidia.so
#
# And the same D-Bus connection where NVIDIA's Vulkan driver is not kept
# loaded: a round that moves the GPU between kernel drivers, which unloads
# it (bare_driver.py's LAVAPIPE_WHY). Its frames are then an unknown
# module's and neither entry above can match, so the key is libdbus, which
# bare_driver.py keeps loaded there for it. Checked against the
# kms-vulkan-bare asan round on schenker's GTX 1050 Ti that starts on
# nouveau (580.196.07, Arch's libdbus 1.16.2 named by its debuginfod):
# vkEnumerateInstanceExtensionProperties -> (the driver, unnamed) ->
# internal_bus_get, one connection each time the GPU moved to NVIDIA's
# driver -- three, 2314 bytes in nine blocks, three direct -- and nothing
# else, twice. aspire's 960M again reported none. Nothing of this project
# calls libdbus, and no backend links it, so no allocation of ours is made
# under one of its frames.
leak:libdbus-1.so

# ---- Mesa's display WSI ---------------------------------------------------
# wsi_display_swapchain_destroy() sets the connector's crtc_id to 0 as the
# display's last swapchain goes, and the next surface query's
# wsi_display_setup_crtc() then assigns connector->formats afresh without
# freeing the list it had: the primary plane's formats, 4 bytes each. On
# the KMS display path that is every mode change -- the surface is made
# again for the new mode -- so one list a change, the last freed with the
# instance. tools/probe/wsidisplay.c shows it with nothing of fenestal in
# it: three rounds, three lists, 156 bytes each on the Pi's vc4 (39
# formats) and 60 on aspire's HD 530 (15; Mesa 26.2.5 both). The laptops'
# rounds never meet it -- their panels offer one mode, and lavapipe takes
# the copy path -- and this desktop's console does: RADV on the RX 580,
# 72 bytes (18 formats) at each mode row. The validation layer saw no
# object of ours left alive, and no driver is given an allocator of ours.
#
# By the function where the driver has debug info -- Arch's, from its
# debuginfod, as aspire's anv named it: vk_zalloc in wsi_display_setup_crtc
# (wsi_common_display.c:2470) under wsi_display_surface_get_formats. Checked
# against the kms-vulkan-bare asan round on this desktop's console (Mesa
# 26.0.8, Ubuntu's RADV with its debug info installed, the driver kept
# loaded by bare_driver.py): this key, 2 leaks, 144 bytes, and nothing else.
leak:wsi_display_setup_crtc
#
# And by the module on the Pi, whose V3DV has none: Arch Linux ARM serves no
# debug info, so its frames are offsets into libvulkan_broadcom.so (kept
# loaded by bare_driver.py) and the module is the narrowest key. Checked
# against the kms-vulkan-bare asan round there: 156 bytes at each of the
# two mode rows, and nothing else.
leak:libvulkan_broadcom.so
